"A bruise is a repair you can watch in color. A bump breaks tiny blood vessels under the skin, and blood leaks out, but the skin is not broken. At first the bruise is red, blue, or purple. As your body breaks the old blood down and carries it away, the bruise turns green and then yellow or brown. In about two weeks it is gone. You cannot make these steps go faster, but you can help: wash a cut with clean water, keep it covered, eat and sleep well, and let the scab do its job. Now start a healing diary with the tool below the model.": "faac608693e8",
"A clot stopped the bleeding.": "6e6e0d88b717",
"Bones follow the same four steps on a slower clock. When a bone breaks, blood pools around the break and forms a clot within a few hours. White blood cells clean up for about a week. Then the body builds a patch: first a soft bridge, like rubber, and then a hard lump of new bone around the break. That takes some weeks, and it is why you wear a cast. The cast does not heal the bone. It only holds the pieces still while the bone heals itself. Finally, over months and years, bone cells trim the lump away until the bone has its old shape again. Young bones do all of this faster than old ones.": "964628e29403",
"Do not pick the scab on your knee.": "9527d69676c5",
"He has a small scar on his chin.": "387bfda247fe",
"I got a bruise when I bumped the table.": "f5f6256fd308",
"My ankle is swollen after the fall.": "d87733565ec6",
"She had a cast on her arm for six weeks.": "cc1a5693cbce",
"Step one: stop the leak. When you are cut, blood vessels are cut too. Within minutes, tiny blood cells called platelets stick to the edges and to one another. Then a net of sticky threads forms and glues everything into a clot. On your skin, the top of the clot dries out and becomes a scab. A scab is your body's own bandage, so try not to pick it.": "747c359fc181",
"Step three: build a quick patch. Under the scab, new blood vessels grow, and soft pink tissue fills the gap. Skin cells creep across from the edges until they meet. This patch is made fast, and it is not very strong. Step four: rebuild it properly. For months, even a year or more, your body replaces the patch with stronger fibers. A deep cut leaves a scar, and a scar never gets quite as strong as the skin was before.": "a708f547332c",
"Step two: clean up. White blood cells, which you met in the lesson about germs, arrive within an hour. They swallow germs, dirt, and dead cells. More blood flows to the area to bring them, and that is why the skin around a cut is red, warm, and a little swollen for a few days. It does not feel good, but it is a sign that the cleanup crew is working.": "f659a6450ff8",
"The cut will heal in about a week.": "f72a850fd215",
"Think about the last time you scraped your knee. It bled, it stung, and a week later the scab fell off and showed new pink skin. Nobody had to tell your knee what to do. A broken toy stays broken, but your body repairs itself, and it follows the same plan every time. The plan has four steps.": "be49ed6a82e4",
